::ENG4_U04_L06_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Passage: "The room emptied quickly after the vote... She did not call the meeting a failure. Not yet." Which response is analysis rather than summary?
{
~A. Maya stayed after a meeting and folded papers.#Incorrect. This only retells what happened.
=B. The passage uses the quiet aftermath of the vote to show Maya interpreting failure as a chance to revise.#Correct. This explains how the passage creates meaning.
~C. The passage includes a room, folders, and lights.#Incorrect. This lists details without explaining function.
~D. Maya was in a hallway after a vote.#Incorrect. This summarizes events.
}
::ENG4_U04_L06_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.05] What is the function of the short sentence "Not yet"?
{
=A. It creates a restrained tone of possibility after disappointment.#Correct. The short sentence shifts the moment from failure toward persistence.
~B. It proves Maya has no opinion about the vote.#Incorrect. The sentence reveals her developing interpretation.
~C. It changes the setting to a different city.#Incorrect. The sentence does not change setting.
~D. It removes the passage's reflective mood.#Incorrect. It strengthens the reflective mood.
}
::ENG4_U04_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which analytical sentence is clearest?
{
~A. The text and Maya and vote because revision maybe.#Incorrect. This is not a clear sentence.
~B. Maya after meeting papers folded tone.#Incorrect. This is fragmented and unclear.
=C. The final sentence gives Maya a reflective voice by comparing a failed vote to a draft that can be revised.#Correct. This sentence clearly identifies choice and function.
~D. The passage is about stuff happening after something.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
}
::ENG4_U04_L06_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] In complex text analysis, why should a writer gather several evidence details before drafting?
{
~A. To copy the passage into the paragraph.#Incorrect. Evidence should support analysis, not replace it.
~B. To avoid making an analytical claim.#Incorrect. Evidence helps form a stronger claim.
~C. To make the response longer without purpose.#Incorrect. More evidence is not useful unless it supports analysis.
=D. To choose the details that best support an interpretation of author choices.#Correct. Gathering and selecting evidence supports focused analysis.
}
::ENG4_U04_L06_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Passage: "A design that only looked impressive was not finished; it had to carry someone safely..." What does this line develop?
{
~A. A comic tone about decoration.#Incorrect. The line is serious, not comic.
=B. The idea that revision is tied to responsibility and function.#Correct. The line connects design revision to practical responsibility.
~C. A claim that appearance is the only purpose of design.#Incorrect. The line argues the opposite.
~D. A setting change from workshop to classroom.#Incorrect. The line does not change setting.
}
